<|start|>assistant to=user<|message|>The piece is a parody / “I’m leaving Rust for C++” rant, and the technical touchstones it uses pin it to the very early-mid 2020s, just after C++20 shipped and while C++23 was on the horizon and C++26 was still speculative.

* C++20 is presented as the current new standard and *modules* is described as “an innovative new feature, and one I’m looking forward to actually being implemented fairly soon.” In practice modules were standardised in 2020 but were only then starting to become usable in real toolchains. That is the 2021-2023 window.
* The author looks forward to C++26 with “stackful coroutines, the ability to embed binary file contents to initialize arrays, a safe range-based for loop, and many other goodies.” C++26 was the active future standard in the 2022-2024 discussion cycle; stackful coroutines and binary embedding were the kinds of proposals being talked about then.
* On the Rust side the author cites “recently landed features such as generic associated types have more of the flavor of making combinations of existing features work as expected rather than bringing any fundamentally new capabilities; the forthcoming ‘type alias impl trait’ is similar.” GATs were stabilised in Rust 1.65 in November 2022 and “type alias impl trait” was the hot RFC / stabilization discussion of late 2022-early 2023. The edition mechanism is invoked as the reason Rust can’t break code, which is the narrative of that period.
* The Linebender projects – Kino, Druid, etc. – were very publicly Rust GUI work in 2022-2023, and the whole “we’re rewriting Linebender in C++” premise is a tongue-in-cheek inversion of the real community discussion.

All of that puts the writing in the 2022-2023 window, and the tone, the hyperbolic community complaints, the AIX/DEC OSF/1 AXP nostalgia and the Hacker News / r/rust “Discuss” footer are classic April Fools satire. The most plausible publication moment is April 1, 2023, with a realistic earliest window of late 2022 to early 2024, essentially the C++20-is-new / C++26-is-future era. [end of text]


